  "not_visible_to_this_read": "A caller who has never touched a ticket AND has no users row at all is in NEITHER table and cannot appear here: a constructed authorizer context, or a non-human identity carrying an Opal firm id that was never written to opal-users-v2. The authorizer mints firmId, so for an address with no PROFILE row the class is NOT determinable from these two tables; it is recorded as class_internal_by_row null, never as false.",
  "findings_beyond_the_counts": [
    "NO PERSON would be locked out by the release. All 7 addresses with no PROFILE row read as non-human or test identities: 6 on the opalinvest.dev domain (battery and promotion identities from earlier rounds) and 1 on opal.invalid, which is the synthetic filer whose row THIS SEAT deleted at 19:10:54Z today under TO-RUN-R4-tr1, by that order's own design.",
    "The release keys on the PRESENCE of a row, not on its status, so the 2 rows with status 'disabled' would still be admitted after it. One of those carries userType 'Dev'; the other carries firmId opal-capital with a non-Opal email domain and no userType. Naming this is not a claim that it is wrong -- the release's rule is the CTO's -- only that 'has a row' and 'is an active internal user' are different populations, and the PRD's ADDENDUM 3 says ACTIVE.",
    "The order's model of who is refused TODAY is a row COUNT (more than one). The count is 0, but that is not the whole set: _profile_scope has a THIRD refusal path at line 391, a malformed allowedFirmIds, and one live row with firmId opal-capital and status active carries that attribute as a STRING of length 10 rather than a list. See the surprise row filed with this read. The same row also carries userType 'service' with a local-part the handler does not know, which would make it UNRESOLVABLE before the scope lookup is reached -- so it is refused either way, and WHICH path fires depends on the userType the authorizer mints, which this read cannot see."
  ]
}
